DTOs — Data Transfer Objects
A DTO is a plain object whose only job is to carry data across a boundary (usually between your API and the outside world).
It's not your domain model, it's not your entity, it's a shape you design for the outside world.

Why is DTO here is useful?
1. Sensitive fields leak
Employee has password. Even with @JsonIgnore, you're one forgotten annotation away from a breach.
With a DTO, password simply doesn't exist in the response class — it can never leak.

2. Any model change becomes a breaking API change
Rename image to imageUrl in Employee? You just broke every client consuming the API. With a DTO,
you rename the field internally and map it to the same JSON key — clients don't notice.

3. You can't shape the response
What if the frontend wants fullName (computed), or wants salary formatted as "$45,000", or wants employeeType:
"FULL_TIME"? You'd have to pollute your domain class with presentation logic. A DTO is for that.

4. Abstract hierarchies don't serialize cleanly
Your Employee is abstract with two subclasses. Returning List<Employee> works via Jackson's runtime introspection,
but the JSON shape is inconsistent (salary for one, hourlyRate for the other). A DTO lets you define a consistent, intentional shape.

